  "instructions_md": "---\nname: rails-security\ndescription: Ruby on Rails security practices for Rails 7.1+ and 8.x apps. Covers production configuration, authentication, authorization, strong parameters, SQL injection, XSS and output escaping, CSRF, file uploads, API security, credentials, untrusted boundaries, and dependency scanning. Use when reviewing or writing Rails authentication, authorization, input handling, uploads, or deployment configuration.\norigin: community\n---\n\n# Rails Security\n\nSecurity practices for Ruby on Rails applications (Rails 7.1+ and 8.x). Rails is secure by\ndefault in most places; the vulnerabilities that reach production usually come from opting\nout of a default rather than failing to add protection. This skill is the \"how.\" For the\ndecisions about which control to reach for, see the Ruby security rules \u2014\n`rules/ruby/security.md` in this repository, installed as `rules/ecc/ruby/security.md`.\n\n## When to Activate\n\n- Writing or reviewing authentication, sessions, or account recovery\n- Adding authorization checks to controllers or views\n- Handling user input, file uploads, or webhook payloads\n- Configuring a Rails app for production\n- Reviewing a PR that touches `config/`, `app/controllers/`, or `Gemfile`\n- Any use of `html_safe`, `raw`, `Arel.sql`, or raw SQL strings\n\n## Production Configuration\n\n```ruby\n# config/environments/production.rb\nRails.application.configure do\n  config.force_ssl = true\n  # Only use when all requests pass through a proxy that rejects or redirects HTTP.\n  config.assume_ssl = true # behind a proxy that terminates TLS\n\n  # Reject requests with unexpected Host headers.\n  config.hosts << \"example.com\"\n  config.hosts << /.*\\.example\\.com/\n\n  config.action_dispatch.cookies_same_site_protection = :lax\n  config.log_level = :info\nend\n```\n\nFilter sensitive values out of logs:\n\n```ruby\n# config/initializers/filter_parameter_logging.rb\nRails.application.config.filter_parameters += %i[\n  passw secret token _key crypt salt certificate otp ssn cvv card_number\n]\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nRails 8 ships an authentication generator that produces session-based auth with password\nreset and metadata tracking:\n\n```bash\nbin/rails generate authentication\n```\n\nUse Devise when you need OAuth, MFA, confirmable, lockable, or multi-model auth. For\nhand-rolled auth, `has_secure_password` handles bcrypt hashing:\n\n```ruby\nclass User < ApplicationRecord\n  has_secure_password\n\n  validates :email, presence: true, uniqueness: { case_sensitive: false }\n  validates :password, length: { minimum: 12 }, allow_nil: true\nend\n```\n\n`authenticate_by` looks up and verifies in one call, so a missing user and a wrong password\ntake the same time:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad: the timing difference reveals whether the email exists\nuser = User.find_by(email: params[:email])\nif user&.authenticate(params[:password])\n\n# Good\nuser = User.authenticate_by(email: params[:email], password: params[:password])\n```\n\nRotate the session on sign-in and on any privilege change:\n\n```ruby\nclass SessionsController < ApplicationController\n  def create\n    user = User.authenticate_by(email: params[:email], password: params[:password])\n    return render :new, status: :unprocessable_entity unless user\n\n    reset_session\n    session[:user_id] = user.id\n    redirect_to root_path\n  end\n\n  def destroy\n    reset_session\n    redirect_to root_path\n  end\nend\n```\n\n### Account recovery\n\nPassword reset tokens must expire, be single-use, and not reveal whether an account exists.\nSigned tokens generated by the model give you both:\n\n```ruby\nclass User < ApplicationRecord\n  generates_token_for :password_reset, expires_in: 15.minutes do\n    password_salt&.last(10) # invalidates the token once the password changes\n  end\nend\n\nuser = User.find_by_token_for(:password_reset, params[:token])\n```\n\nRender the same response whether or not the address matched.\n\n## Authorization\n\nAuthentication is not authorization. Every action that touches a record must check that the\ncurrent user may act on it.\n\n```ruby\n# Bad: any signed-in user can load any invoice\n@invoice = Invoice.find(params[:id])\n\n# Good: scope through the owner\n@invoice = current_user.invoices.find(params[:id])\n```\n\nFor anything past ownership, use a policy object (Pundit, Action Policy, or CanCanCan):\n\n```ruby\nclass InvoicePolicy < ApplicationPolicy\n  def update?\n    user.admin? || record.user_id == user.id\n  end\nend\n\nclass InvoicesController < ApplicationController\n  def update\n    @invoice = Invoice.find(params[:id])\n    authorize @invoice\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n```\n\nFail closed \u2014 verify that authorization ran rather than trusting each action to remember:\n\n```ruby\nclass ApplicationController < ActionController::Base\n  include Pundit::Authorization\n\n  after_action :verify_authorized, except: :index\n  after_action :verify_policy_scoped, only: :index\nend\n\n# Public actions have no record to authorize, so exempt them explicitly.\nclass SessionsController < ApplicationController\n  skip_after_action :verify_authorized\nend\n```\n\n## Strong Parameters and Mass Assignment\n\nNever pass raw `params` to a model. Permit an explicit list:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad: a crafted request can set admin\nUser.new(params[:user])\n\n# Good\ndef user_params\n  params.require(:user).permit(:email, :name)\nend\n```\n\nRails 8 adds `expect`, which raises on a malformed structure instead of returning an empty\nhash:\n\n```ruby\ndef user_params\n  params.expect(user: [:email, :name])\nend\n```\n\nAttributes such as `admin`, `role`, `user_id`, and `account_id` should never appear in a\npermit list driven by request data. Assign them explicitly from the session or a checked\npolicy.\n\n## SQL Injection\n\nActive Record parameterizes by default. Injection appears when a string is built by hand:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad\nUser.where(\"email = '#{params[:email]}'\")\nOrder.order(\"#{params[:sort]} #{params[:dir]}\")\n\n# Good\nUser.where(email: params[:email])\nUser.where(\"email = ?\", params[:email])\n```\n\n`order`, `pluck`, `group`, and `select` accept raw SQL and are not sanitized. Allowlist the\ninput rather than escaping it:\n\n```ruby\nSORTABLE = { \"created_at\" => \"created_at\", \"total\" => \"total\" }.freeze\nDIRECTIONS = { \"asc\" => \"ASC\", \"desc\" => \"DESC\" }.freeze\n\ncolumn = SORTABLE.fetch(params[:sort], \"created_at\")\ndirection = DIRECTIONS.fetch(params[:dir], \"DESC\")\nOrder.order(Arel.sql(\"#{column} #{direction}\"))\n```\n\n`LIKE` needs its own escaping, since `%` and `_` are wildcards:\n\n```ruby\nterm = User.sanitize_sql_like(params[:q])\nUser.where(\"name LIKE ?\", \"%#{term}%\")\n```\n\n## XSS and Output Escaping\n\nERB escapes interpolated values by default. Every bypass is security-sensitive:\n\n```erb\n<%# Bad: renders attacker-controlled markup %>\n<%= @comment.body.html_safe %>\n<%= raw @comment.body %>\n\n<%# Good: escaped %>\n<%= @comment.body %>\n\n<%# Good: allowlisted markup when the field is genuinely rich text %>\n<%= sanitize @comment.body, tags: %w[p br strong em a], attributes: %w[href] %>\n```\n\n`html_safe` does not sanitize \u2014 it marks a string as already safe and skips escaping. Only\ncall it on strings you constructed.\n\nInterpolating into JavaScript needs the matching escape, not the HTML one:\n\n```erb\n<script>\n  const payload = <%= raw @data.to_json %>;              // Bad\n  const payload = <%= raw json_escape(@data.to_json) %>; // Good\n</script>\n```\n\n`link_to`'s href accepts `javascript:` URLs, so validate user-supplied links:\n\n```ruby\nuri = URI.parse(url) rescue nil\nsafe = uri.is_a?(URI::HTTP) # URI::HTTPS subclasses URI::HTTP\n```\n\nSet a Content Security Policy:\n\n```ruby\n# config/initializers/content_security_policy.rb\nRails.application.configure do\n  config.content_security_policy do |policy|\n    policy.default_src :self\n    policy.script_src  :self\n    policy.object_src  :none\n    policy.frame_ancestors :none\n  end\nend\n```\n\n## CSRF\n\nRails enables CSRF protection for new apps. Keep it on, and do not exempt actions to make a\nclient work:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad: disables protection for every action in the controller\nskip_before_action :verify_authenticity_token\n```\n\nIf an endpoint genuinely cannot carry the token \u2014 a third-party webhook, say \u2014 authenticate\nit another way (signature verification) and scope the exemption to that action alone.\n\nAPI-only controllers inheriting from `ActionController::API` have no cookie session, so CSRF\ndoes not apply; token or session-cookie APIs still need it.\n\n## File Uploads\n\nThe `content_type` a client sends is attacker-controlled, and `blob.content_type` is not a\nsubstitute for it. Active Storage derives that column through `Marcel::MimeType.for`, which\ntreats the client's declared type as one candidate alongside magic-byte detection and keeps\nit when the bytes match nothing known \u2014 so opaque bytes declared `image/png` are stored as\n`image/png` with `identified?` true. Inspect the stored bytes yourself:\n\n```ruby\nmodule Uploads\n  Rejected = Class.new(StandardError)\n\n  MAGIC = {\n    \"application/pdf\" => \"%PDF-\".b,\n    \"image/png\" => \"\\x89PNG\\r\\n\\x1a\\n\".b,\n    \"image/jpeg\" => \"\\xFF\\xD8\\xFF\".b\n  }.freeze\n  ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS = %w[.pdf .png .jpg .jpeg].freeze\n  MAX_BYTES = 10.megabytes\n\n  def self.verified!(blob)\n    # Fail closed: an object that is missing or still pending cannot be inspected.\n    raise Rejected, \"could not be verified\" unless blob.service.exist?(blob.key)\n    raise Rejected, \"is too large\" if blob.byte_size > MAX_BYTES\n\n    extension = File.extname(blob.filename.to_s).downcase\n    raise Rejected, \"extension not allowed\" unless ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS.include?(extension)\n\n    head = blob.service.download_chunk(blob.key, 0...8)\n    raise Rejected, \"type not allowed\" unless MAGIC.any? { |_mime, magic| head.start_with?(magic) }\n\n    blob\n  end\nend\n```\n\nThe check reads from storage, so it has to run where the bytes already exist. A form upload\nis not yet in storage when model validations run \u2014 Active Storage uploads on save \u2014 so\nupload explicitly first, and attach only what passed:\n\n```ruby\n# Form upload\nblob = ActiveStorage::Blob.create_and_upload!(io: params[:file], filename: params[:file].original_filename)\ndocument.file.attach(Uploads.verified!(blob))\n\n# Direct upload: the client already stored the object, so verify before the record\n# points at it.\ndocument.file.attach(Uploads.verified!(ActiveStorage::Blob.find_signed!(params[:file])))\n```\n\nAttach to a private service \u2014 a public bucket makes every upload world-readable regardless\nof the checks above.\n\n```ruby\nhas_one_attached :file, service: :private_documents\n```\n\n`rails_blob_url` and `url_for(attachment)` are stable references that never expire; they\nredirect to a short-lived service URL, so anyone holding one keeps access. Serve\nuser-scoped files from your own controller that authorizes each request, with\n`config.active_storage.draw_routes = false`, which removes every default Active Storage\nroute; apps using direct uploads need a replacement route:\n\n```ruby\nget \"documents/:id/file\", to: \"document_files#show\", as: :document_file\n# Rails' direct-upload endpoint has no authentication of its own. This example uses a\n# hand-rolled session sign-in; see Devise or the Rails 8 generator for their equivalents.\nconstraints ->(req) { req.session[:user_id].present? } do\n  post \"uploads\", to: \"active_storage/direct_uploads#create\", as: :rails_direct_uploads\nend\n\nclass DocumentPolicy < ApplicationPolicy\n  def show?\n    record.user_id == user.id\n  end\nend\n\nclass DocumentFilesController < ApplicationController\n  def show\n    document = current_user.documents.find(params[:id])\n    authorize document, :show?\n    redirect_to document.file.blob.url(expires_in: 5.minutes), allow_other_host: true\n  end\nend\n```\n\nNever interpolate a user-supplied path into `send_file`:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad: path traversal\nsend_file Rails.root.join(\"storage\", params[:name])\n```\n\n## API Security\n\nRate-limit authentication and other abusable endpoints. Action Controller ships\n`rate_limit` from Rails 7.2; use `rack-attack` on 7.1 or for app-wide throttling:\n\n```ruby\nclass SessionsController < ApplicationController\n  rate_limit to: 10, within: 3.minutes, only: :create\nend\n```\n\nVerify webhook signatures with a constant-time comparison:\n\n```ruby\nclass WebhooksController < ApplicationController\n  skip_before_action :verify_authenticity_token, only: :create\n\n  def create\n    expected = OpenSSL::HMAC.hexdigest(\"SHA256\", ENV.fetch(\"WEBHOOK_SECRET\"), request.raw_post)\n    given = request.headers[\"X-Signature\"].to_s\n\n    unless ActiveSupport::SecurityUtils.secure_compare(expected, given)\n      return head :unauthorized\n    end\n\n    event_id = JSON.parse(request.raw_post).fetch(\"id\")\n    delivery = WebhookDelivery.create_or_find_by!(provider: \"billing\", delivery_id: event_id)\n    return head :ok if delivery.processed_at?\n\n    ProcessWebhookJob.perform_later(delivery.id, request.raw_post)\n    head :ok\n  end\nend\n```\n\n```ruby\nadd_index :webhook_deliveries, %i[provider delivery_id], unique: true\n```\n\nThe job marks `processed_at` when it finishes. Jobs retry, so `perform` must be\nidempotent for the same reason. Requires a stable id in the signed body.\n\nKeep CORS narrow \u2014 never reflect an arbitrary origin alongside credentials:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad\norigins \"*\"\n\n# Good\norigins \"https://app.example.com\"\n```\n\nSerialize responses explicitly so new columns do not leak:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad: ships every column, including ones added later\nrender json: @user\n\n# Good\nrender json: @user.as_json(only: %i[id name created_at])\n```\n\n## Secrets and Credentials\n\nSecrets belong in Rails credentials, environment variables, or a secret manager:\n\n```bash\nbin/rails credentials:edit --environment production\n```\n\n`config/credentials/production.key` must stay out of version control. Read secrets through\n`Rails.application.credentials` or `ENV.fetch` \u2014 `ENV[...]` returns `nil` and fails silently\nwhen a variable is missing:\n\n```ruby\n# Bad\nENV[\"STRIPE_SECRET\"]\n\n# Good\nRails.application.credentials.dig(:stripe, :secret)\nENV.fetch(\"STRIPE_SECRET\")\n```\n\nIf a key is ever committed, rotate it. Removing the commit does not un-expose it.\n\n## Untrusted Boundaries\n\nController params are not the only entry point. Job arguments, webhook payloads, Action\nCable messages, and Turbo Stream targets all carry outside data:\n\n- Pass record IDs to jobs and re-scope inside `perform`; a queued job runs without the\n  original request's session.\n- Authorize in `ApplicationCable::Connection` and in each channel's `subscribed`.\n- Never build a Turbo Stream target from user input.\n\n```ruby\nclass ExportJob < ApplicationJob\n  def perform(user_id, invoice_id)\n    user = User.find(user_id)\n    invoice = user.invoices.find(invoice_id) # scoped, not Invoice.find\n    # ...\n  end\nend\n```\n\n## Dependency Scanning\n\nRun these when the lockfile changes, and in CI:\n\n```bash\nbundle exec bundle-audit check --update\nbundle exec brakeman --no-progress\n```\n\nBrakeman flags the patterns above statically. Treat new warnings as blocking, and record\ndeliberate exceptions in `config/brakeman.ignore` with a reason rather than lowering the\nconfidence threshold.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\n- [ ] `force_ssl` and `config.hosts` set in production\n- [ ] Sensitive parameters filtered from logs\n- [ ] Sessions rotated on sign-in and privilege change\n- [ ] Every record lookup scoped to the current user or checked by a policy\n- [ ] No permit list contains a role, ownership, or account column\n- [ ] No raw SQL string interpolation; `order` and `pluck` inputs allowlisted\n- [ ] Every `html_safe` and `raw` reviewed\n- [ ] CSRF exemptions scoped to specific actions with another auth mechanism\n- [ ] Uploads validated against stored bytes, not `blob.content_type`, and fail closed when the object is unavailable\n- [ ] Auth endpoints rate-limited; webhook signatures compared in constant time\n- [ ] No secrets in version control; master key excluded\n- [ ] `bundle-audit` and `brakeman` clean\n\n## Related Skills\n\n- `rails-patterns` \u2014 the architectural patterns these controls sit inside\n- `security-review` \u2014 secure-by-default review patterns across languages\n- Ruby security rules (`rules/ruby/security.md`, installed as `rules/ecc/ruby/security.md`)\n  \u2014 the decisions and when-to-use guidance this skill implements\n",
